/**
 * staged/c_types/c_types.tks   (namespace `teko::c_types` — NOT COMPILED YET)
 *
 * ============================================================================================
 * POR QUE ESTE ARQUIVO ESTÁ FORA DE `src/` (staged, não morto)
 * ============================================================================================
 *
 * Este módulo é o desenho CANÔNICO de `teko::c_types` e está inteiro e correto. Ele só não pode
 * morar em `src/` AINDA, e a razão é a escada, não o conteúdo: quem tipa `src/` é o SEED, um
 * binário 0.3.0 já congelado, e este módulo consome builtins que a MESMA carga acabou de criar —
 * `teko::mem::region_buf` (novo) e o `teko::mem::as_cstr` RETIPADO de `ptr` opaco para
 * `ptr<byte>`. Pedir que `src/` os use é pedir que o passado conheça o futuro; o seed rejeita, e
 * rejeitou: os cinco portões ficaram vermelhos em 994bcc4 no mesmo defeito, em todos os hosts.
 *
 * A carga irmã (`#arena_size`) já tinha enunciado a regra e a respeitou: uma carga que ensina um
 * builtin novo ao compilador é ADITIVA — `src/` NÃO adota nesta carga, porque o seed precisa
 * continuar construindo o gen1. O que esta carga entregou de fato e permanece em `src/` é o lado
 * COMPILADOR (as assinaturas em `checker/scope.tks`, os emissores em `codegen/codegen.tks`) — esse
 * lado compila sob o seed porque é só código novo, não uso novo. O que saiu foi só o CONSUMIDOR.
 *
 * NADA quebra com a saída, e isso foi medido, não suposto: `teko::c_types` não tem um único
 * importador. Todas as sete fixtures da carga (`q192`, `q194`, `q195`, `q197`, `q199`, `c62`..`c65`)
 * carregam uma CÓPIA LOCAL declarada como tal no próprio doc-comment — é assim que elas exercitam
 * os builtins novos através do gen1, que os tem. As únicas menções a `teko::c_types` fora daqui são
 * prosa de doc-comment.
 *
 * CONDIÇÃO DE REENTRADA, uma só e verificável: o primeiro degrau cujo SEED já traga `as_cstr`
 * devolvendo `ptr<byte>` E `region_buf`. Nesse degrau isto volta para `src/c_types/` sem edição —
 * o arquivo já está na grafia final, sem forma transitória, porque meia-verdade em `src/` custou
 * um vagão inteiro. Confira com:
 *     grep -n 'name == "as_cstr"\|name == "region_buf"' src/checker/scope.tks
 * lido a partir do commit do seed vigente, não do topo.
 *
 * ============================================================================================
 *
 * The C ABI's primitive vocabulary, named once and used everywhere an `extern fn` binds a raw C
 * library — `docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md` is the design this module implements;
 * every claim below cites it by section. The owner's own framing governs the whole module:
 * `unsafe` here is a CAPABILITY ENABLER (it turns on pointers and sigils), not a hazard sticker,
 * and the safety comes from every conversion being TOTAL — every input, including the bad ones,
 * has a defined result (§1).
 *
 * THE TWELVE ALIASES (§5.1) are each exactly what the owner ratified: *"type c_int = i32; não
 * está mentindo"* — a `type` alias is TRANSPARENT (`resolve.tks:794`), so a `c_int` IS an `i32`,
 * not a wrapper pretending to be one. Every alias below carries NO address and is therefore SAFE
 * (no `unsafe` stamp): assigning through one, casting one, comparing one — all exactly as if the
 * aliased primitive had been written directly.
 *
 * THE ADMISSION RULE (§5.0), so this list is derivable rather than curated: an entry enters only
 * if (1) a named, real caller demands it (an extern in this tree, a sibling cargo's design, or a
 * `teko_rt` binding) and (2) its Teko mapping is the SAME on every target the compiler can emit
 * (`arm64-macos`, `arm64-linux`, `x86_64-linux`, `x86_64-windows`). A type that fails rule 2 is
 * not deferred — it is NOT EXPRESSIBLE, and §5.2
 * says so for each excluded C type:
 *
 * | C type                              | why it is OUT |
 * |--------------------------------------|---------------|
 * | `long` / `unsigned long`             | 64-bit on LP64, 32-bit on LLP64 — one alias would lie on half the targets, and `#os` cannot guard a TYPE declaration. Bind the exact width instead (`c_int`/`c_longlong`) |
 * | `size_t`/`ssize_t`/`ptrdiff_t`       | 64-bit on every target the compiler emits, but Teko has no target-width integer to express that portably. Bind `c_ulonglong` for the 64-bit targets |
 * | `intptr_t`/`uintptr_t`               | already `uptr`, one word — a second name would be a synonym |
 * | `uptr \| null`                       | NOT buildable: `Uptr` has no spare bit-pattern (`0` is data, not a hole), so it lowers to a two-word tagged struct where C expects one. Nullability at the boundary is always `ptr<T> \| null` (§5.4.3) |
 * | `void` / `void *`                    | already `void` / the opaque `ptr` (or `ptr<byte>`) — a second name would be a synonym (§5.1, [PIN-2]) |
 * | a C function pointer                 | Teko cannot construct one to hand to C (`extern_type_ok` rejects `Func`); belongs to `cargo/20-concorrencia-adiantada`'s `cabi fn` design |
 * | `pthread_t`                          | a different *kind* per platform (`unsigned long` on POSIX, `HANDLE` on Windows) — bind per-`#os` on the FUNCTION, which `#os` DOES allow |
 * | struct-by-value C types              | `extern_type_ok` refuses a struct without an `ExternBody`, and a struct crossing an extern boundary is a C compile error even where admitted (`time.tks:21-34`) |
 * | `wchar_t`/`char16_t`/`char32_t`       | no caller anywhere in this tree or a sibling cargo; Teko's text is UTF-8 end to end |
 * | `long double`, `_Complex`, bit-fields, unions, varargs | no caller; each is a separate ABI project (varargs is `star-ref-and-ffi-0.3.1.md` §4.3's) |
 * | `errno`                               | a macro over a TLS lvalue, not a type — Teko has no macros; bind `__errno_location()`/`_errno()` as a plain `extern fn` instead |
 *
 * `void`, BOTH of its C meanings, get NO ENTRY [PIN-2]: `void f(…)` is already the one-word
 * `void` return, proven on a raw libc extern; `void *` is a POINTER — `ptr<byte>` when Teko reads
 * or writes the bytes at that address, the opaque `ptr` when the address is a token Teko only
 * ever hands back to the same foreign library, and NEVER `uptr` (§5.3: `uptr` IS `uintptr_t`, and
 * mapping `void *` to it would be a silent ABI pun — the same pun class the concurrency cargo
 * rejected under M.3 for `fn_addr -> u64`).
 *
 * NULLABILITY AT THE BOUNDARY [PIN-3, RATIFIED] is `ptr<T> | null`, ALWAYS — declared per symbol
 * from the C library's own contract (never derived; §5.4.2), because roughly half of any C API's
 * pointers must NOT carry it (NULL is undefined behaviour for them) and a blanket `| null` would
 * be as dishonest as the blanket `u64` it replaces. `uptr | null` is never buildable and never
 * correct: an integer has no spare bit-pattern, so it lowers to a two-word tagged struct where C
 * expects one machine word — a reviewer who finds it anywhere in the tree should treat it as a
 * defect (§5.4.3).
 *
 * @since 0.3.1
 */

/**
 * The C `char` octet — `setenv`/`unsetenv`/`getenv`'s payload octets, `memset`/`memcpy`'s buffers.
 * Plain `byte`: C's `char` signedness is platform-defined, so the alias picks the Teko primitive
 * that carries no sign claim at all.
 */
pub type c_char = byte

/** The C `signed char` — the one case where an API declares signedness EXPLICITLY, not the platform. */
pub type c_schar = i8

/** The C `unsigned char` — the unsigned twin of `c_schar`, for an API that declares it explicitly. */
pub type c_uchar = u8

/** The C `short` — 16-bit on every emittable target (e.g. `time.tks`'s `wall_offset_minutes: i16`). */
pub type c_short = i16

/** The C `unsigned short` — symmetry with `c_short`; a port number in a future socket binding. */
pub type c_ushort = u16

/**
 * The C `int` — THE owner's own worked example (`type c_int = i32`). `setenv`'s `overwrite` flag
 * and its return value, `memset`'s fill `value`, `abs`, `getpid` — the 32-bit signed C integer
 * that is 32-bit on every target the compiler emits.
 */
pub type c_int = i32

/** The C `unsigned int` — symmetry with `c_int`; a C API's flag word. */
pub type c_uint = u32

/** The C `long long` — the 64-bit signed C integer that is 64-bit EVERYWHERE, unlike `long`. */
pub type c_longlong = i64

/**
 * The C `unsigned long long` — every SIZE argument in this tree's raw-libc probes today
 * (`aligned_alloc`'s `alignment`/`size`, `memset`/`memcmp`/`memcpy`'s `count`).
 */
pub type c_ulonglong = u64

/**
 * The C `float` (IEEE binary32) — admitted for symmetry with `c_double`. No caller yet in this
 * tree; kept because C fixes `float` as binary32 and Teko fixes `f32` identically, so the entry
 * can never become wrong on any target (`docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md` §5.1).
 */
pub type c_float = f32

/** The C `double` (IEEE binary64) — `libm` is the first C library any FFI reaches; `f64` is 8 bytes everywhere. */
pub type c_double = f64

/**
 * The C99 `_Bool` — one byte, and Teko's `bool` is `PrimKind::Bool`; needed the moment a C API
 * returns or accepts `_Bool` directly (as opposed to the C89 `int`-as-boolean idiom, which is `c_int`).
 */
pub type c_bool = bool

/**
 * A NUL-terminated C string OWNED BY TEKO — the safe-side carrier of the one invariant a
 * `const char *` has and a Teko `str` does not: `ptr[len] == 0`
 * (`docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md` §7.1). It is a HANDLE, not a value, and the ABI
 * forces that: a `const char *` parameter is ONE machine word, and the checker refuses a struct in
 * an `extern` signature (C7.1a, `typer.tks:5109`), so this type NEVER crosses the boundary — only
 * the single word `c_string_ptr` extracts from it does.
 *
 * It is `unsafe type … = struct { … }` — a NOMINAL declaration, and NOT an alias to a bare
 * structural shape (`unsafe type c_string = ptr<byte>` was considered and rejected for exactly
 * this reason). A `type` alias is TRANSPARENT (`resolve.tks:794`): it resolves THROUGH to its
 * target, so the alias's own name never survives resolution — an `unsafe` modifier on a
 * STRUCTURALLY-typed alias used to be SILENTLY INERT (the stamp is looked up on a `Named`,
 * `resolve.tks:1158`) until `cargo/20-unsafe-alias-rejeitado` closed that hole into a hard
 * compile error. `c_string`'s nominal shape was chosen independently of that fix landing — a
 * `struct { … }` body IS a `Named` declaration either way, so the stamp was never at risk here —
 * and it is what makes a `c_string` field in a safe struct trip the SAME gate
 * `c55_unsafe_field_in_safe_struct` pins, and a `c_string` in a safe signature trip
 * `reject_unsafe_signature_contagion` (`collect.tks:39`).
 *
 * LIFETIME: a `c_string` owns bytes INSIDE a region it did not open, so it is NOT `#must_free` and
 * has no destructor — a bump region frees in bulk, never per-object. The free obligation lives on
 * the REGION handle (`teko::mem::unsafe::Arena`, already `#must_free`), never here. Which region is
 * NAMED at construction and never inferred: see `c_string_for_call` and `c_string_in_arena`.
 *
 * @see docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md §7
 * @since 0.3.1
 */
pub unsafe type c_string = struct {
    /**
     * The base address of a `len + 1` octet buffer whose final octet is 0. The buffer lives in the
     * region named at construction and is valid exactly as long as that region is.
     *
     * `ptr<byte>` E NÃO o `ptr` opaco: §5.3 manda o ponteiro TIPADO exatamente quando o Teko lê ou
     * escreve os octetos no endereço, que é este caso — o terminador é escrito por quem constrói e
     * relido por `teko::mem::str_from_c`. O opaco fica reservado para o endereço que o Teko só
     * devolve à mesma biblioteca estrangeira sem nunca desreferenciar.
     */
    ptr: ptr<byte>

    /**
     * The payload length in octets, EXCLUDING the terminator — i.e. the length of the Teko `str`
     * this was built from. It is deliberately NOT "what C will see": if the source `str` held an
     * embedded NUL, C stops at that NUL and reads FEWER octets than `len` (owner ruling: the
     * truncation is the developer's, and there is nothing the compiler can do). Anything that needs
     * "what C saw" must read back THROUGH the terminator with `teko::mem::str_from_c`, never
     * through this field.
     */
    len: u64
}

/**
 * Copies `s` into a fresh NUL-terminated buffer in the ENCLOSING region and hands back the owning
 * `c_string` — tier 1. The result is valid for exactly as long as the enclosing region, so it is
 * correct when the C side READS DURING THE CALL AND FORGETS (POSIX `setenv`/`unsetenv` copy both
 * arguments; `fopen`, `printf`, `strlen` read and return). **If the C side RETAINS the pointer, this
 * is the wrong constructor** — use `c_string_in_arena` and the failure becomes a lifetime the
 * developer controls instead of a dangling read.
 *
 * TOTAL: every `str` produces a `c_string`; there is no failure path and no `| error`. An EMBEDDED
 * NUL in `s` is copied verbatim, so the C side sees a TRUNCATED string — the octets up to the first
 * NUL and no further. That is stated, not prevented: owner ruling, *"cabe ao desenvolvedor e não ao
 * compilador, C verá truncado e não há o que fazer"*. The buffer still holds all `len` octets and
 * `len` still counts them; only C's view is short. The empty `str` yields a valid 1-octet buffer
 * holding just the terminator, which is what C expects for `""`.
 *
 * @param str s  the string to copy across the boundary
 * @return c_string  an owning handle to a NUL-terminated copy in the enclosing region
 * @see docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md §7.2
 * @since 0.3.1
 */
pub unsafe fn c_string_for_call(s: str): c_string {
    c_string { ptr = teko::mem::as_cstr(s); len = s.len }
}

/**
 * Copies `s` into a fresh NUL-terminated buffer in the DEV-NAMED region `a` and hands back the
 * owning `c_string` — tier 2. The result is valid until `teko::mem::free(a)`, so it is the
 * constructor for a C API that RETAINS the pointer (`putenv`, a registered callback context, any
 * library that stores the string). Leaking is already barred: `Arena` is `#must_free`, so a path
 * that drops `a` without freeing it is a COMPILE error (`src/mem/unsafe/arena.tks`).
 *
 * TOTAL under exactly the same terms as `c_string_for_call` — same embedded-NUL truncation, same
 * absence of a failure path. The ONLY difference is which region owns the octets, and that is the
 * entire reason the two constructors are separate names rather than one with a flag: the lifetime
 * is visible at the call site (M.5).
 *
 * @param teko::mem::unsafe::Arena a  the manual region that will own the copy until it is freed
 * @param str s  the string to copy across the boundary
 * @return c_string  an owning handle to a NUL-terminated copy inside `a`
 * @see docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md §7.2
 * @since 0.3.1
 */
pub unsafe fn c_string_in_arena(a: teko::mem::unsafe::Arena, s: str): c_string {
    c_string { ptr = teko::mem::region_buf(a.region, s.len + 1, s); len = s.len }
}

/**
 * Extracts the single machine word an `extern fn` actually receives — the base address of the
 * NUL-terminated buffer. This is the ONE hop across the boundary, and it is deliberately a named,
 * greppable function rather than an implicit coercion: the `c_string` itself can never be an extern
 * parameter (C7.1a rejects a struct, `typer.tks:5115`), so something must do the extraction, and
 * making it visible is the whole of M.5.
 *
 * @param c_string s  the owning handle
 * @return ptr<byte>  the base address of `s`'s NUL-terminated buffer, valid for `s`'s region
 * @see docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md §7.4
 * @since 0.3.1
 */
pub unsafe fn c_string_ptr(s: c_string): ptr<byte> {
    s.ptr
}
